raptormark: name lifted functions by address alone, not discovery order.

GetUniqueLiftedFuncName appended a monotonic counter immediately in front of the
guest address that already identifies the function:

    <sym>_____<unique_i64++>_<vma>

The counter follows discovery order over the whole binary, so a program with a
differently-sized executable shifts the index of every library function after it.
Measured on two programs fused from one rootfs with a shared library layout,
1,527 functions sat at IDENTICAL guest addresses and 0 of 1,527 received the same
name -- the counter alone forced them apart.

That defeats cross-program reuse. A library's lifted code is identical between
programs once its address is fixed (internal/fuse closure layout), and identical
code should get an identical name so the partition cache can serve it once and so
the linker can collapse the duplicate definitions.

Dropping the counter is safe because disasm_funcs is keyed by address: there is
exactly one lifted function per guest address, so <sym>_<vma> is already unique
within a module. The address was already present in the name; the counter added
instability and nothing else.

unique_i64 is removed rather than left unused, since it is a private member and
-Wunused-private-field would fire.
--- a/lifter/TraceManager.h
+++ b/lifter/TraceManager.h
@@ -38,8 +38,7 @@
  public:
   virtual ~AArch64TraceManager(void) = default;
   AArch64TraceManager(std::string target_elf_file_name)
-      : elf_obj(BinaryLoader::ELFObject(target_elf_file_name)),
-        unique_i64(0) {}
+      : elf_obj(BinaryLoader::ELFObject(target_elf_file_name)) {}
 
   void SetLiftedTraceDefinition(uint64_t addr, llvm::Function *lifted_func);
   std::string AddRestDisasmFunc(uint64_t addr);
@@ -84,6 +83,4 @@
   uintptr_t entry_point;
   std::string target_arch;
 
- private:
-  uint64_t unique_i64;
 };
--- a/lifter/TraceManager.cpp
+++ b/lifter/TraceManager.cpp
@@ -155,8 +155,7 @@
 
 std::string AArch64TraceManager::GetUniqueLiftedFuncName(std::string func_name, uint64_t vma_s) {
   std::stringstream lifted_fn_name;
-  lifted_fn_name << func_name << "_____" << std::to_string(unique_i64++) << "_" << std::hex
-                 << vma_s;
+  lifted_fn_name << func_name << "_____" << std::hex << vma_s;
   return lifted_fn_name.str();
 }
 
